# =============================================================================
# Build context exclusions.
#
# There was none, so every `docker build` sent the whole repository — including
# `node_modules`, every `dist/`, the real `.env` and the git history — to the
# daemon. Two consequences, one slow and one wrong:
#
#   SLOW: the context is hundreds of megabytes that are discarded immediately.
#
#   WRONG: each Dockerfile runs `pnpm install` and THEN copies `packages/` and
#   `apps/<app>/`. Without this file those copies bring the host's
#   `packages/*/node_modules` and `apps/*/node_modules` over the ones the image
#   just installed. On a Windows host those are pnpm junctions, which do not
#   resolve inside a Linux container at all.
#
# `.env` is not copied by any Dockerfile, but it is excluded here too: a build
# context is not a place to put secrets on the assumption that nothing reads
# them.
# =============================================================================

# Dependencies and build output — every image installs and builds its own.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/.turbo
**/.vite

# Secrets and local state.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
_local/
infra/caddy-data/
infra/caddy-config/

# Version control and editor metadata.
.git
.gitignore
.github
.vscode
.idea
.claude

# Test output, coverage and scratch directories.
coverage/
**/.playwright-cli
test-results/
playwright-report/

# Documentation and design material: none of it is needed to build a runnable
# image, and DIVE1 alone is over a megabyte.
*.md
docs/
specs/
assets1/
tests3/
